# =============================================================================
# Molaro et al. (2019) T = -20 C -- alpha_c = 0.1, open chamber, production
#
# ALPHA_C = 0.1, and it is NOT a free knob here. Two independent bounds pin it:
#
#   FROM BELOW (their data). The attachment-only limit -- infinitely fast vapour
#   transport, so no geometric assumption -- needs
#       alpha_c >= sqrt(2 pi m/kT) * v_n / (d0' * dK) = 0.139 integrated
#   to reproduce their measured dr/dt. Per-interval it spans 0.05-0.42.
#
#   FROM ABOVE (saturation + cost). Neck growth is already transport-limited at
#   alpha_c = 0.1 (Lambda = beta_HK*D_v/rho = 0.29), so going all the way to the
#   physical ceiling alpha_c = 1 buys only 1.25x more rate. Raising alpha_c also
#   TIGHTENS the K&P interface-width bound, since L* = D*beta_HK ~ 1/alpha_c: at
#   alpha_c = 1 the mean-heat ceiling drops to 5.9e-8 and the mesh grows 19x.
#
# So the model tops out near 50 % of Molaro's observed neck growth at any
# alpha_c. The residual is the surface-diffusion share this model does not have
# -- see docs/molaro_validation_synthesis.md sec. 4. Recompute with
#     python preprocess/estimate_alpha_c_molaro.py
#
# HUMIDITY 1.0000 -- saturated control: isolates neck growth from shrinkage
# Derived, not guessed. Quasi-steady sublimation from the pair to the wall with
# both resistances in series:
#     1 - hum0 = |v_n| * (beta' + l_eff/D_v) / (rho_vs/rho_i),  l_eff = a(1-a/L)
# Target |v_n| = 6.2544e-10 m/s, the LARGE grain's surface recession from a
# least-squares fit to their Fig. 11 diameters (-2.93 %/78 min, S/N 4.4). That
# reproduces the -3 % their caption quotes. The caption's -4 % for the SMALL
# grain is not in the same table -- endpoint -0.69 %, minimum -2.40 %, fit
# -0.89 %, all inside its own +-2 % uncertainty -- so fit the large grain and
# report the small one.
#
# At L/a_eff = 3 that gives hum0 = 0.99728. The grid spans the ~3x uncertainty
# in the envelope. NOTE the previously fitted h = 0.998 does NOT transfer: it
# was calibrated on the 121 um domain, and 1 - hum0 is nearly alpha_c-
# independent but strongly L-dependent (0.00035 at L/a = 1.08 -> 0.00272 at 3).
# Recompute for any other domain with
#     python preprocess/estimate_vapor_bc_molaro.py --L-over-a <k>
#
# The wall undersaturation outweighs the curvature difference between the two
# grains (d0*(2/R_sm - 2/R_lg) = 7.9e-6) by 343x, so BOTH grains sublimate at
# nearly the same rate. The differential is not a usable ripening signal.
#
# VAPOUR IC needs no change: SetNodeFields writes rho_v = rho_vs*[hum0*(1-phi)
# + phi], and the quasi-steady profile establishes in L^2/D_v ~ 6e-3 s --
# instantaneous on this run.
#
# T_FINAL 2.2e+04 s -- this is OUR clock, not theirs. We start at a zero neck and
# the model grows slower than the experiment, so t_final is set by how long OUR
# neck takes to cross their range, not by their 78 min.
#
# Rescaled from the repo's own CALIBRATED figure: the alpha_c = 1.341e-2 arms
# needed ~28 ks from tangency to reach their last measured neck (fitted on a
# committed run, see molaro_T-20_h1.00_15h_a1.34e-2_dirichlet.opts). Time scales
# with the TOTAL resistance, not with beta alone -- we are in the mixed regime:
#     t(0.1)/t(1.341e-2) = (beta'(0.1) + rho/D_v) / (beta'(1.341e-2) + rho/D_v)
#                        = (0.0733 + 0.2515) / (0.5469 + 0.2515) = 0.407
#     their first neck 32.81 um  ->  t* ~ 0.42 h
#     their last  neck 64.78 um  ->     ~ 3.16 h
# Scaling by beta alone would give 1.04 h and cut the run short. 6 h is ~1.9x
# margin on 3.16 h. The 2 h arms cover their first three points.
#
# DTMAX = 0.8 * dt_CFL, derived rather than inherited.
# -dtCFL caps max|dphi| per step at dphimax = 0.2 using the MEASURED field
# change. The logistic profile has max|grad phi| = 1/(4 eps), so
#     dt_CFL = dphimax * 4 * eps / v_n = 0.8 * eps / v_n
# dtmax is a STATIC backstop, so it needs one v_n. dt_CFL spans five decades
# over this run because v_n does -- picking the early value would pin dtmax at
# ~0 and the run would never advance:
#     neck width   r[um]   v_n[m/s]     dt_CFL[s]   0.8*dt_CFL[s]
#      1.03 (IC)    0.52   8.23e-06          0.0            0.0
#     32.81         16.41  2.61e-09         79.1           63.3
#     48.00         24.00  5.94e-10        348.4          278.7
#     64.78         32.39  1.55e-10       1337.6         1070.1
# Reference = their LAST measured neck, the slowest point we still need
# accurate and where dt is largest, giving -dtmax 1.07e3.
# Regenerate with: python preprocess/estimate_alpha_c_molaro.py
#
# READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING IT. 1070 s is 5.35x the 200 s in
# inputs/solver.opts, which is an EMPIRICALLY TESTED value from earlier runs.
# So this does not tighten anything -- it hands the limiting back to -dtCFL,
# which computes the same cap per step from measured |dphi| rather than from a
# growth-law estimate. That is the right division of labour, but it means the
# tested guardrail is no longer the binding one. Rung 1 of
# studies/molaro_2019/RUNBOOK.md exists to check exactly this on the cheapest
# arm: if plots/timestep.png shows dt pinned at 1070 s with no "Interface-CFL
# cap" lines in the log, dtCFL is NOT doing its job and this must go back to
# 200 s. The tail is only ~50 steps either way, so nothing is lost by being
# careful here.
#
# The earlier Molaro files pinned -dtmax to 1.1*tau_sub instead. tau_sub is the
# Allen-Cahn RELAXATION time, not a stability limit for an implicit solver; at
# this eps that is 8.6 s, 23x tighter than the tested default, and ~6300 steps
# of pure waste.
#
# COST: the interface advances 0.8*eps = 0.21 um per step at the CFL cap, and
# the neck grows 0 -> 32.4 um, so expect a few hundred steps, not thousands.
# Budget ~220 core-h per 6 h arm at dom3 -- check plot_timestep.py on the first
# run to see whether dtCFL or dtmax is actually binding.
#
# -beta_sub0 is deliberately absent: it is IGNORED under -alpha_pointwise 1,
# where beta_sub is rebuilt per quadrature point from alpha_c.
#
# Pair with: inputs/geometry/molaro/molaro_2D_L*um_eps0.26um_axisym_T-20pair_tangent_dom*.opts
# Measure:   python postprocess/neck_width.py <run_dir> --axisym
#            python postprocess/grain_shrinkage.py <run_dir>
# =============================================================================

-t_final 2.2e+04
-temp -20.0
-humidity 1.0000
-grad_temp0 0.0,0.0,0.0

-flag_BC_Tfix 1                         # isothermal reservoir (the cold plate)
-flag_BC_rhovfix 1                      # rho_v pinned at 1.0000 * rho_vs(T)

-alpha_pointwise 1                      # mob_sub/alph_sub rebuilt per quad point
-alpha_model 0                          # 0 = constant alpha_c
-alpha_c0 1.0e-1
-alpha_lo 1.0e-3
-alpha_hi 1.0e-1

-d0_sub0 1.0166e-9                      # physical capillary length at -20 C

-dtmax 1.07e3                           # 0.8 * dt_CFL at their last neck; see above
-dtCFL 1
-dtCFL_dphimax 0.2

-t_out_log 80
-t_out_log_t0 1.0e2
